  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1 DESCRIPTION...\n\nAn upper-level ridge axis will continue to move across the\nregion today as we transition into southwest flow aloft. This\npattern will keep the weather quiet the rest of today and\ntonight with the exception of some valley fog tonight into\nThursday morning. Lows will mainly be in the lower 50s to lower\n60s. \n\nUpper-level trough then starts it's approach tomorrow, leading \nto increasing chances of showers and thunderstorms in the \nafternoon into the night. As we continue to move further into \nthe arena of the CAMs, the forecast continues to trend drier. As\nof now, NBM probabilities for at least a half inch of rain only\nmaxes out in the ~40 to 60 percent range and that's mainly \nconfined to the White Mountains. That being said, many of the\nCAMs show multiple rounds of showers and storms moving through \ntomorrow afternoon and evening along and ahead of an approaching\ncold front. With PWATs close to normal or slightly above normal\nfor this time of year, some brief heavy downpours will be \npossible under the strongest convection but it will be very hit \nor miss. 12z HRRR soundings are advertising some instability \nwith 500 to 1000 J/kg of MLCAPE but deep layer shear will be \nmarginal in the 25 to 35 knot range. Thus, a few thunderstorms \nthat move into the forecast area could become strong with small \nhail and gusty winds, mainly in New Hampshire. Storms will be \ndiminishing in the evening as we lose diurnal heating but \nshowers may linger over some locations through the night. \n\nAnother wave will interact with the cold front on Friday,\npotentially leading to some more widespread showers and a few\nrumbles of thunder but coverage is still a bit uncertain as some\nof the longer range CAMs are showing minimal activity and some\nare showing quite a bit. Precipitation chances then diminish \nrather quickly as move closer to sunset and the front passes \nthrough in the evening. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2 DESCRIPTION...\nThe front continues to trend faster in the latest guidance with\nit looking like most of the area will be able to clear out \nearly on in the night and get in on radiational cooling. I still\nhave my doubts about southeastern New Hampshire and the \nimmediate coast and have left their low temperatures in the mid\nto upper 50s, but the rest of the interior should easily be \nable to realize the low 50s. North of the mountains will be \nclear the longest and should be able to radiate down into the \nmid to upper 40s. While dry air is going to be quickly filling \nin behind the front, this radiational cooling is likely to \nproduce valley fog. High pressure slides eastward across the \nregion through the weekend which should result in mostly sunny \nskies and high temperatures generally in the upper 70s and low \n80s. Clouds thicken late Sunday as high pressure departs and an \nupper low moving through Quebec makes a close pass. All global \nmodels have a good handle on this now and looks like the result \nwill be a front draped along the International Border and \nCentral Maine that would provide forcing for showers through at \nleast Monday. Tuesday is a little more uncertain as we get \nridging aloft, but a second upper low is going to be following a\ntrack similar to the early week one and will likely drag \nanother front through midweek. The global models have different \nideas on the exact track of this second system, but having a \nchance of showers for the first part of next week seems \nreasonable given this setup. This will also come with cooler \ntemperatures just in time for the beginning of meteorological \nFall.",
